Biochemical mechanisms of drug toxicity:
Normally drug -> non-toxic metabolites -> excreted
When drug breaks into reactive metaboloties, it can lead to tox. Intrinsic tox is dose dependent, idosyncratic tox is not dose dependent. 

Intrinsic tox - Acetaminophen breaks down to intermediate causing oxidative stress in a dose dependent manner. 
Alcohol acts as a substrate for the p450 enzyme and causes more oxidative stress. 

Idisyncratic - Example of trovofloxacin- tvx (antibiotic) causing liver injury in an inflammatory condition. 
Assess TRV efects on ALT levels following LPS administration. 

Ways to assess toxic metabolites (coupled to dose) 
-GSH trapping by LC-MS/MS: 
Treat with drug and trap with vehicle or something that will trap the intermediate -> assess by LC-MS/MS
-TDI

- Multifactorial approach (coupled to dose + ways to assess cellular effects) - 
Hazard matrix showing covalent binding burden against in-vitro panel score -> measure hazard
In vitro panel with HepG2, MRP2, BSEP etc. 

In vitro methods: 
3D hepatic culture -> assess ATP levels for viability, albumin secretion for function



